email us at security@electronjs.org\n\n# ASAR Integrity Bypass via resource modification\n\nModerate\n\nVerteDinde published GHSA-vmqv-hx8q-j7mg Sep 3, 2025\n\n## Package\n\nelectron (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 35.7.5 || >= 36.0.0-alpha.1 < 36.8.1 || >= 37.0.0-alpha.1 < 37.3.1 || >= 38.0.0-alpha.1 < 38.0.0-beta.6\n\n## Patched versions\n\n35.7.5, 36.8.1, 37.3.1, 38.0.0-beta.6\n\n## Description\n\n### Impact\n\nThis only impacts apps that have the `embeddedAsarIntegrityValidation` and `onlyLoadAppFromAsar` fuses enabled. Apps without these fuses enabled are not impacted.\n\nSpecifically this issue can only be exploited if your app is launched from a filesystem the attacker has write access too. i.e. the ability to edit files inside the `resources` folder in your app installation on Windows which these fuses are supposed to protect against.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nThere are no app side workarounds, you must update to a patched version of Electron.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n  * `38.0.0-beta.6`\n  * `37.3.1`\n  * `36.8.1`\n  * `35.7.5`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nelectron/docs/glossary.md at main · electron/electron · GitHub (https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/docs/glossary.md)\nciteturn40search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; ASAR integrity is a security feature that validates the contents of your app's ASAR archives at runtime. ... Context isolation is a security measure in Electron that ensures that your preload script cannot leak privileged Electron or Node.js APIs to the web contents in your renderer process.\n\n# Glossary\n\nThis page defines some terminology that is commonly used in Electron development.\n\n### ASAR\n\nASAR stands for Atom Shell Archive Format. An asar archive is a simple `tar`-like format that concatenates files into a single file. Electron can read arbitrary files from it without unpacking the whole file.\n\nThe ASAR format was created primarily to improve performance on Windows when reading large quantities of small files (e.g. when loading your app's JavaScript dependency tree from `node_modules`).\n\n### ASAR integrity\n\nASAR integrity is a security feature that validates the contents of your app's ASAR archives at runtime. When enabled, your Electron app will verify the header hash of its ASAR archive on runtime. If no hash is present or if there is a mismatch in the hashes, the app will forcefully terminate.\n\nSee the ASAR Integrity guide for more details.\n\n### code signing\n\nCode signing is a process where an app developer digitally signs their code to ensure that it hasn't been tampered with after packaging. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOverview · electron/electron · GitHub (https://github.com/electron/electron/security)\nciteturn40search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; To report a security issue, please use the GitHub Security Advisory \"Report a Vulnerability\" tab. ... Learn more about advisories related to electron/electron in the GitHub Advisory Database\n\n# Security: electron/electron\n\n## Security\n\nReport a vulnerability\n\n### SECURITY.md\n\n# Reporting Security Issues\n\nThe Electron team and community take security bugs in Electron seriously. We appreciate your efforts to responsibly disclose your findings, and will make every effort to acknowledge your contributions.\n\nTo report a security issue, please use the GitHub Security Advisory \"Report a Vulnerability\" tab.\n\nThe Electron team will send a response indicating the next steps in handling your report. After the initial reply to your report, the security team will keep you informed of the progress towards a fix and full announcement, and may ask for additional information or guidance.\n\nReport security bugs in third-party modules to the person or team maintaining the module. You can also report a vulnerability through the npm contact form by selecting \"I'm reporting a security vulnerability\".\n\n## Escalation\n\nIf you do not receive an acknowledgement of your report within 6 business days, or if you cannot find a private security contact for the project, you may escalate to the OpenJS Foundation CNA at `security@lists.openjsf.org`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpdate deprecated npm dependencies · Issue #4464 · NousResearch/hermes-agent · GitHub (https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/issues/4464)\nciteturn40search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week; While they don't break functionality, they include packages with known memory leaks and security advisories. ...     npm warn deprecated lodash.isequal@4.5.0: Use require('node:util').isDeepStrictEqual instead.\n\n# Update deprecated npm dependencies #4464\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\nUpdate deprecated npm dependencies#4464\n\nCopy link\n\nLabels\n\nP3 Low — cosmetic, nice to haveLow — cosmetic, nice to havearea/install-update Installer, updater, packaging, wheels, doctorInstaller, updater, packaging, wheels, doctorcomp/cli CLI entry point, hermes_cli/, setup wizardCLI entry point, hermes_cli/, setup wizarddependencies Pull requests that update a dependency filePull requests that update a dependency filetype/refactor Code restructuring, no behavior changeCode restructuring, no behavior change\n\n## Description\n\nheyramzi\n\nopened on Apr 1, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n## Summary\n\nSeveral npm dependencies are deprecated and produce warnings during `npm install`. While they don't break functionality, they include packages with known memory leaks and security advisories.\n\n## Warnings during `npm install`\n    \n    `npm warn deprecated inflight@1.0.6: This module is not supported, and leaks memory.\n    npm warn deprecated lodash.isequal@4.5.0: Use require('node:util').isDeepStrictEqual instead.\n    npm warn deprecated whatwg-encoding@3.1.1: Use @exodus/bytes instead.\n    npm warn deprecated prebuild-install@7.1.3: No longer maintained.\n    npm warn deprecated rimraf@3.0.2: Rimraf versions prior to v4 are no longer supported.\n    npm warn deprecated glob@7.2.3: Old versions contain widely publicized security vulnerabilities.\n    npm warn deprecated glob@10.5.0: Same as above.\n    `\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\nPackage  | Action\n--- | ---\n`glob`  | Update to v11+\n`rimraf`  | Update to v4+\n`inflight`  | Remove (leaks memory)\n`whatwg-encoding`  | Replace with `@exodus/bytes`\n`lodash.isequal`  | Replace with `node:util.isDeepStrictEqual`\n`prebuild-install`  | Check if still needed by native deps\n\nSome of these may be transitive (from other dependencies), in which case bumping the direct dependency versions should resolve them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · electron/asar · GitHub (https://github.com/electron/asar/releases)\nciteturn40search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Releases: electron/asar ... This commit was created on GitHub.com and signed with GitHub’s verified signature.\nView all tags\n\nImage: @electron-npm-package-publisher electron-npm-package-publisher released this 21 Jul 11:23\n\nv4.2.1\n\n`0959a13`\n\nThis commit was created on GitHub.com and signed with GitHub’s verified signature.\n\nGPG key ID: B5690EEEBB952194\n\nVerified\n\nLearn about vigilant mode.\n\n## 4.2.1 (2026-07-21)\n\n### Bug Fixes\n\n  * add circular symlink detection and depth limit protection (#423) (fd0fcd8)\n  * add DoS validation for asar archive headers and file entries (#419) (51cd188)\n  * add header validation for ASAR archives (#421) (00e5293)\n  * add offset validation to readFileSync to prevent out-of-bounds reads (#420) (97c09cc)\n  * add path validation to prevent directory traversal attacks (#418) (de0689b)\n  * compute integrity over transformed bytes when a transform is applied (#450) (8c9be01), closes #277\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBrowsing